summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Follow-up of chronic problems.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Carolyn M. is a 49-year-old female who presents for follow-up of her chronic problems.

The patient reports that she is doing okay. She has been experiencing some flare-ups of her osteoarthritis, primarily in her elbows. She notes that she has been sitting at the desk more in the office. She denies any other joint pain. She experiences some numbness in her hands when she is typing for long periods of time.

The patient reports that she has a history of gout. The last episode was 3 months ago. She was treated with medication and had no further flare-ups. She has had no further episodes of gout since.

The patient also has a history of psoriasis. She reports that it is under control and she has not had any flare-ups.

The patient is right-handed. She reports that she has been experiencing some pain and inflammation in her right elbow. She denies any left elbow pain. She reports that she has been experiencing some pain and inflammation in her right elbow. She denies any left elbow pain. She reports that she has been experiencing some pain and numbness in her right elbow. She denies any left elbow pain. She reports that she has been experiencing some pain and numbness in her right elbow. She denies any left elbow pain.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

Musculoskeletal: Reports osteoarthritis in her elbows. Denies other joint pain.

VITALS

Vital signs are normal.

PHYSICAL EXAM

Neck
- General Examination: No cervical lymphadenopathy.

Cardiovascular
- Auscultation of Heart: Regular rate and rhythm.

Respiratory
- Auscultation of Lungs: Clear bilaterally.

Musculoskeletal
- Examination of right elbow: Edema and inflammation of the right olecranon, tenderness and effusion present. Pain with pronation and supination. Pain with flexion and extension of the right arm.

Abdomen
- Examination: Soft.

Lower Extremities
- Examination: No edema.

RESULTS

Labs are reviewed in detail. Autoimmune panel is within normal limits. ESR is within normal limits. Uric acid is within normal limits.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

1. Gout.
- Medical Reasoning: The patient has a history of gout. The last episode was 3 months ago. The uric acid level is within normal limits. The patient has been compliant with her treatment and has had no further episodes of gout.
- Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed the nature of gout and the importance of maintaining her current medication regimen.
- Medical Treatment: Continue colchicine 0.6 mg twice a day as needed for flare-ups.

2. Osteoarthritis of the right elbow.
- Medical Reasoning: The patient reports that she is experiencing some flare-ups of her osteoarthritis in her elbows. The examination reveals edema, inflammation, and tenderness of the right olecranon with effusion. She has a family history of osteoarthritis.
- Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed the nature of osteoarthritis and the importance of maintaining a healthy weight and using physical therapy.
- Medical Treatment: We ordered a right elbow x-ray to assess the extent of the arthritis. We will refer her for physical therapy based on the results of the x-ray. We will prescribe meloxicam 15 mg once a day for pain and swelling.

3. Psoriasis.
- Medical Reasoning: The patient reports that her psoriasis is under control. She has been compliant with her treatment.
- Medical Treatment: Continue clobetasol for her scalp.

Patient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.
