summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Yearly follow-up for asthma, allergic rhinitis and food allergy.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Emily Hernandez is a 20-year-old female who presents for her yearly follow-up. The patient was last seen in April 2020. She reports she was switched from Symbicort to Wixela due to insurance changes. She uses Zyrtec in the spring, summer, and fall, which tends to work well. She also has a known peanut allergy, but tolerates almonds and cashews.

The patient states she has been doing well. She uses her inhaler once in the morning and once at night. She states she rarely uses her albuterol inhaler and only when she is exerting herself. She notes she has been trying to use her daily inhaler less because she is trying to get into the military. She states she is able to go to the grocery store and walk without any issues. She only uses her inhaler before a workout.

She denies any urgent care or ER visits for breathing over the past year. She denies any episodes of needing prednisone for breathing issues.

The patient is planning to enlist in the military early spring of 2022. She notes she will need to prove she can operate without an inhaler to be accepted.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

Constitutional: Denies fever, weight loss, or night sweats.
Respiratory: Denies cough or wheezing. Denies hemoptysis.
ENT: Denies nasal congestion or sinus issues.
Allergic/Immunologic: Denies rash or itching. Denies any issues with tree nuts other than peanuts.

PHYSICAL EXAM

Respiratory: Auscultation of Lungs: Clear to auscultation bilaterally. No wheezing, rales, or rhonchi.
Cardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm. No murmurs, gallops, or rubs.
ENT: Ears: Bilateral cerumen impaction. Mouth: No lesions or petechiae. Nose: Bilateral nasal septum deviation.

RESULTS

Spirometry: FEV1: 85% of predicted. FVC: 95% of predicted.

ASSESSMENT

Emily Hernandez is a 20-year-old female with a history of asthma, allergic rhinitis, and a peanut allergy, who presents for a yearly follow-up.

Asthma.
• Medical Reasoning: Her spirometry shows a FEV1 of 85% of predicted. She reports good control with Wixela. She rarely uses her albuterol inhaler. She denies any urgent care or ER visits over the past year. We discussed the option of stepping down to an inhaler, but she prefers to continue Wixela.

Allergic Rhinitis.
• Medical Reasoning: She continues to use Zyrtec as needed in the spring, summer, and fall. She denies any issues with tree nuts other than peanuts.

Peanut Allergy.
• Medical Reasoning: She continues to avoid peanuts. She has tolerated almonds and cashews in the past. She has had no recent exposure to peanuts and has been cautious.

PLAN

Asthma.
• Medical Treatment: Continue Wixela 250/50 mcg, 1 puff twice a day. Order Alvesco 80 mcg, 2 puffs twice a day, quantity: 1 inhaler, 30 doses. Order Albuterol, 90 mcg/inhalation, 1 inhaler, 2 refills. Repeat spirometry in 4 months.
• Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed the option of stepping down to an inhaler. She will continue to use her daily inhaler less as she is trying to get into the military.

Allergic Rhinitis.
• Medical Treatment: Continue Zyrtec 10 mg as needed in the spring, summer, and fall.
• Patient Education and Counseling: Continue to avoid peanuts and tree nuts.

Peanut Allergy.
• Medical Treatment: Continue to avoid peanuts. Order Epipen, quantity: 2, for emergency use.

Patient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.

INSTRUCTIONS

The patient will follow up in 4 months with ACT and spirometry, sooner if needed. }}<|eot_id|>
