summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Return visit for IBS with functional abdominal pain, nausea, and vomiting.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Brittany Edwards is a 32-year-old female who was last seen in August of 2019 with flares of abdominal pain who was diagnosed with IBS. She was treated with Bentyl. She had a previous prescription for Reglan that she received from the emergency room which she used as needed for nausea. She was instructed to start a low dose fiber supplement, such as Citrucel, daily, probiotics to help with gas and bloating, Bentyl up to four times daily for intestinal cramping, and to stop Reglan and use Zofran as needed. Neuromodulators such as Elavil and Buspar were discussed but not started.

The patient reports that she has been going out to eat on Friday nights and Saturday nights and has had some spicy foods or spicy shrimp. Her gut does not always appreciate these foods.

She has been experiencing cramping and nausea. She states she is starting to notice bowel movements between 2-3 times a day, and they are formed stools. She tends to have diarrhea after she goes out to eat. She does not think it is the food or alcohol, but rather the combination. She does not think the pasta bothers her. She does not think the alcohol bothers her either. She prefers to drink Patron or dark liquor. She drinks 2 drinks every now and then. She does not drink a martini often. She does not think the mixer bothers her.

When she is not eating out or drinking, what she does on a daily basis does not keep her regular. When she does flare up, it is not as bad. She has not used Citrucel regularly. She is still having cramping and sharp pains, so she is avoiding heavy foods. She did have some fried chicken today. She did not have any problems.

The patient states that she cannot go without fried chicken at least once in a while.

PAST HISTORY

Medical
Irritable Bowel Syndrome

SOCIAL HISTORY

She enjoys going out to eat on Friday nights and Saturday nights.

MEDICATIONS

Bentyl 20 mg as needed 30-45 minutes prior to eating out at a restaurant.

PHYSICAL EXAM

Respiratory
Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally.

Cardiovascular
Regular rate and rhythm.

Gastrointestinal
The abdomen is soft, non-tender. No masses or organomegaly. Normal active bowel sounds.

ASSESSMENT

Irritable Bowel Syndrome (IBS) and functional abdominal pain, aggravated by high stress, poor eating habits, and poor sleeping habits.

PLAN

I have encouraged dietary and lifestyle modifications. I have also encouraged her to start a neuromodulator such as Elavil 25 mg at bedtime for IBS. She can use Bentyl 20 mg as needed 30-45 minutes prior to eating out at a restaurant. She can also use Zofran if needed. I have encouraged her to reduce greasy and fatty foods and alcohol consumption. I have asked her to follow up in one month by email over MyChart regarding any side effects and issues with Elavil or if adjustments are needed.

INSTRUCTIONS

We discussed the diagnosis of IBS today. All of her questions were answered. }}<|eot_id|>
