summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Follow-up for adult hydrocephalus.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Christopher Watson is a pleasant and cooperative 86-year-old male who presents to the clinic today with his daughter, Theresa. He has a history of progressive gait impairment, cognitive impairment, and decreased bladder control for the past 8-10 years. He was started on a spinal catheter protocol in June 2020 and underwent shunt surgery on 08/01/2020. A Medtronic Strata programmable shunt in the ventricular peritoneal configuration was placed and programmed at a level 2.0.

The patient reports that he has not been hospitalized since his shunt surgery 3 months ago. He notes that his walking has improved, and he is now able to walk to the dining room without assistance. He still has times where he requires help. He reports that his bladder control has improved but is now stable and he is wearing diapers. He denies any issues with sores or decreased/painful urination. He reports that his cognitive abilities have also improved and he no longer feels "lost" in conversations.

The patient denies any headaches or pain from the shunt. He also denies any abdominal pain.

PAST HISTORY

Medical
Hydrocephalus.

SOCIAL HISTORY

Retired teacher.

FAMILY HISTORY

Daughter.

MEDICATIONS

Spinal catheter protocol.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

Constitutional: Denies fever or unintentional weight loss.
Genitourinary: Endorses urinary incontinence and wearing a diaper. Denies sores, decreased or painful output.
Neurological: Endorses walking impairment and cognitive impairment. Denies headaches, recent falls, or hallucinations.
Psychiatric: Denies depression, excessive worrying, or mood swings.

VITALS

Blood pressure: 124/80
Heart rate: 64
Respirations: 18
Weight: 174 pounds
Pain: 0/10

PHYSICAL EXAM

Neurological
Mental Status Exam: MMSE: 26/30 (spelling 25/30, fund of knowledge 26/30). Slight improvement from initial score of 23/30 (spelling) and 24/30 (calculations). Able to get 2 of 3 memory words with cuing, and the third with multiple choice. Previous test showed unable to remember any memory words with cuing without multiple choice and only one with multiple choice.
Tinetti Assessment Tool: Gait score: 6-8/12 without assistive device, balance score: 12/16, total score: 18/20-20/28. Slightly improved from initial score of 15-17/28.
Cranial nerves: Pupils equal. EOMI. Face symmetric. No dysarthria. Motor: Normal bulk and strength. Coordination: Slow finger to nose.

RESULTS

CT of the head 10/15/2020 shows a frontal horn span at the level of the foramen of Monro of 4.6 cm with a third ventricular contour that is flat with a span of 10 mm. By my reading, there is a tiny amount of blood in the right frontal region with a tiny subdermal collection. This was not noticed or noted by the radiologist who stated any extra-axial fluid collections. There is also substantial small vessel ischemic change.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

Adult hydrocephalus.
Gait impairment.
Urinary incontinence and urgency.
Cognitive impairment.

The patient remains stable on his current shunt settings. The CT scan from 10/15/2020 shows a tiny amount of blood in the right frontal region with a tiny subdermal collection. I do not believe this is causing symptoms and it was not noted by the radiologist.

I recommend leaving the shunt as is for another 2-3 months. We will re-evaluate at that time. The patient has improved significantly and it is unlikely that his movement speed will improve to the level needed to pass a driver safety evaluation. He may enroll in a driver safety program without my recommendation and we can continue to watch for improvement.

All questions were answered. }}<|eot_id|>
