summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Follow-up for hypertension.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Amanda Taylor is a 72-year-old female who presents for follow-up of hypertension. She has a history of significant alcohol use disorder and mild hypercholesterolemia. At her last visit on 04/16/2021, her blood pressure was 130/90. After presenting at 150/100 today, hydrochlorothiazide 12.5 mg by mouth once daily and lisinopril 20 mg by mouth once daily were prescribed. CMP and CBC were essentially unremarkable.

The patient reports struggling with alcohol cessation. She had been drinking 4 to 5 times a week of hard alcohol. She is willing to stop drinking. The patient is taking half of the lisinopril dose due to feeling lethargic and tired. She is taking the full dose of hydrochlorothiazide. She denies chest tightness, shortness of breath, or any other symptoms. She is checking her blood pressure at home and reports morning readings of 130-145/95-100 and evening readings of 150/100. She takes her medications in the morning.

PAST HISTORY

Medical
Hypercholesterolemia.

SOCIAL HISTORY

She last smoked on 12/25/2017. She drinks alcohol 4 to 5 times a week. She has not had a drink since Monday. She received her COVID-19 vaccine on 02/09/2021.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

Constitutional: Denies fever, chills, weight loss, or fatigue.
Respiratory: Denies cough, shortness of breath, or wheezing.
Cardiovascular: Denies chest pain, palpitations, or edema.
Gastrointestinal: Denies nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, or constipation.
Genitourinary: Denies dysuria, frequency, or hematuria.
Neurological: Denies headaches, dizziness, or syncope.
Musculoskeletal: Denies joint pain or muscle aches.
Skin: Denies rashes or lesions.

PHYSICAL EXAM

Constitutional: Alert, pleasant, in no acute distress.
Respiratory: Clear to auscultation bilaterally. Unlabored breathing.
Cardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm. No murmurs, gallops, or rubs. No JVD. CVA tenderness absent. No edema.
Neurological: No focal deficits. No tremors.
Skin: No jaundice or cyanosis. Skin is moist.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

Alcohol use disorder.
• Medical Reasoning: She is drinking alcohol 4 to 5 times a week to excess.
• Medical Treatment: She would like to start Naltrexone which we have previously discussed. She will take the injectable form and start on 05/17/2021. We reviewed side effects and risks. The potential benefit of alcohol cessation would be highly beneficial to her for many reasons.

Hypertension.
• Medical Reasoning: Her blood pressure has been uncontrolled today at 150/100. She is taking lisinopril 10 mg daily and hydrochlorothiazide 12.5 mg daily. Quite possibly her blood pressure will improve completely with cessation of alcohol. Her target BP is 130-140/70-80.
• Medical Treatment: Continue lisinopril 10 mg daily and hydrochlorothiazide 12.5 mg daily.

INSTRUCTIONS

The patient will follow up with me in the week of 06/14/2021, which is likely around the time of her second Naltrexone injection. She was advised to upgrade her influenza vaccination and Shingrix.

Patient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan. }}<|eot_id|>
