Tyler Nelson is a 72-year-old male who presents today for follow-up of his rheumatoid arthritis. He has been experiencing right knee pain and swelling. The pain has been present for the past 2 months. He rates his pain as 6 out of 10. He also reports that his left leg has been swollen. The patient states that he has been experiencing this pain and swelling daily. He states that the pain and swelling have impacted his ability to walk and to sit for long periods. He has been compliant with his medication regimen. He has been wearing compression stockings but reports that they have not helped with the swelling. He received a steroid injection in his right knee last time he was here, which provided temporary relief. He has not had any relief since that time.

1. Right knee pain and swelling.
   - Medical Reasoning: The patient is experiencing right knee pain and swelling, which has been present for the past 2 months. He has tried compression stockings with no relief.
   - Patient Education and Counseling: I advised the patient to try to reduce his salt intake, elevate his legs when possible, and to wear compression stockings. I also recommended that he follow up with his private care doctor if the swelling does not improve.
