summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Bilateral knee pain.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Sophia Adams is a pleasant 24-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of bilateral knee pain. The onset of her pain began approximately 6 months ago. The patient denies any specific event that caused the pain. She describes the pain as a deep achy pain located behind her kneecaps. She also notes that she can hear what seems like crepitus in her knees. The pain is exacerbated by activities such as getting from a seated position to standing and after watching TV. The patient states that the pain improves with rest.

The patient has tried ice, Ace wrap, and over-the-counter NSAIDs such as ibuprofen and Aleve. She denies taking any other medications. She states that she is active and enjoys running, typically 5 to 6 miles per day, but she can no longer run half a mile without pain.

Ms. Adams denies any previous fractures or injuries to her knees. She states she has never broken a bone, except for a fracture to her arm when she was in the 6th grade. She denies redness or swelling in her knees.

SOCIAL HISTORY

Patient reports she works from home and tries to stay active. She enjoys running outdoors.

MEDICATIONS

Patient reports taking ibuprofen and Aleve.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

Musculoskeletal: Reports bilateral knee pain, crepitus, and tenderness to palpation of the retinaculum.

VITALS

Vitals are normal.

PHYSICAL EXAM

Respiratory: Regular rate and rhythm, no murmurs.
Musculoskeletal: Normal gait, strength 3/5 for abduction of the legs bilaterally, remainder of muscle strength 5/5 in the lower extremities. Examination of the knees: No erythema or ecchymosis. No effusion. Tenderness to palpation of the bilateral retinaculum. Positive patellar grind test. Pain with squatting. Lachman, anterior and posterior drawer, and McMurray tests are negative bilaterally. Neurological: Patella and Achilles reflexes are symmetrical.

RESULTS

X-rays of the bilateral knees were reviewed and revealed no fractures or osteoarthritis.

ASSESSMENT

Bilateral knee pain, consistent with patellofemoral pain syndrome.

PLAN

I have discussed the diagnosis and treatment options with the patient. We discussed that her symptoms are consistent with patellofemoral pain syndrome. I recommended physical therapy to help strengthen the lower extremities and correct any incorrect running mechanics. I advised her to take ibuprofen or anti-inflammatory as needed for pain relief. I also advised her to take it easy from running for now, but we will continue to assess her progress with each visit.

INSTRUCTIONS

The patient will follow up with me soon. }}<|eot_id|>
