summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Left knee pain.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Angela Johnson is a pleasant 29-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of left knee pain. The onset of her pain began 1 week ago when she was running around with her 5-year-old niece. She states she felt a pop in her knee at the time of injury. The patient denies any previous trauma to the knee. She states her pain is located on the medial and lateral aspect of her knee. The patient states she has been limping since the injury. The patient states she has been taking Tylenol for pain and wrapping her knee with kinesiology tape. She denies taking ibuprofen as she has an ulcer. She states the Tylenol helps a little bit. The patient states she is unable to bear weight on her left leg.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

Musculoskeletal: Reports left knee pain.

PHYSICAL EXAM

Examination of the left knee: There is edema present. There is effusion. There is moderate range of motion. There is pain with flexion and extension of the knee. Negative Lachman's test. Negative varus and valgus stress test.

RESULTS

X-rays of the left knee were obtained and reviewed today. There are no fractures or bony abnormalities.

ASSESSMENT

Left knee pain.

PLAN

After reviewing the patient's examination and radiographic findings today, I have had a lengthy discussion with the patient in regards to her current symptoms. I have explained to the patient the nature of her diagnosis. I have recommended that we treat the patient conservatively. I have recommended that we start the patient on a pain medication that is not an NSAID. I have prescribed the patient Tramadol 50 mg, oral, twice a day, as needed for pain. She can also take Tylenol for breakthrough pain. I have recommended that we place the patient in a knee immobilizer for the next week to keep the knee from moving and to reduce some of the inflammation that she is seeing. I have recommended that she ice her knee 3 to 4 times a day for 20 to 30 minutes at a time. I have also advised her that she should not run for the next 3 weeks.

INSTRUCTIONS

The patient will follow up with me in 2 weeks if she is not improving. }}<|eot_id|>
